# ============================================================================
# Keep every committed byte identical to the working tree
# ============================================================================
# This machine has core.autocrlf=true (the Git for Windows default), which
# normalises CRLF to LF in the index and hands CRLF back on checkout. That is
# wrong for this project for three concrete reasons:
#
#   * _audit/original.sha256 hashes the frozen original's EXACT bytes. Under
#     normalisation a fresh clone would fail `sha256sum -c` on every line, and
#     the manifest is the only integrity record the project has.
#   * ch*.js are CRLF on disk and were edited with CRLF-aware single-line
#     anchors, so the on-disk line endings are part of what was tested.
#   * Simulation_Notes.pdf contains bytes that look like text; a normalising
#     checkout would rewrite them and corrupt the PDF.
#
# `-text` disables conversion everywhere, so what is committed is what was
# verified, on this checkout and on any other.
* -text

# Explicit, though `-text` above already covers them.
*.pdf binary
*.jpg binary
